1.text:
In the air the ionization current is a measure of the energy which is put
into the air in the beam

     $\dot E_{air}=W\, \dot N_{i}=W\,I/e$

is the power which is put into the air in the geometrical cross section
of the beam

* beam at the focus 
$w_{x}=0.30\;{\rm mm}=3.0\times 10^{-2}\,{\rm cm}$,
$w_{y}=6.0\;{\rm mm}=6.0\times 10^{-1}\,{\rm cm}$  

$A=w_{x}w_{y}=1.8\times 10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}$

$L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$,
$\rho_{a}=1.293\times 10^{-3}\ {\rm g\,cm^{-3}}$

$m_{a}= \rho_{a}AL=1.293\times10^{-3}\times 1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1
      =3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}$,
is the mass of air which is seen by the beam.

With the air $W$ value $W\approx 33.97\;{\rm eV}=5.44\times10^{-18}\ {\rm J}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}= \frac{W}{e}I=
\frac{33.97\,{\rm eV}}{1.602\times10^{-19}{\rm C}}\;2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}
\approx 6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}

Hence the air kerma rate which is actually measured in the chamber is

\begin{equation*}
\dot k_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{air}}{m_{a}}
          =\frac{I\,W}{e\,m_{a}}
          \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
          \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ of air kerma at the focus.
In terms of the photon energy fluence,

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}= \Phi_{E}\;\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\;m_{a}\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
\Phi_{E}= \frac{\dot E_{air}}{(\mu_{en}/\rho)m_{a}}\approx5.9\times10^{-7}
{\rm J\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}
so that for a material with the same $(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ as air

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{s}= \Phi_{E}\Big(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Big)_{t}
          =\frac{I\,W}{e\,m_{a}}\,
            \frac{(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}}{(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}}
\end{equation*}
is the instantaneous surface dose rate.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$

$\dot D_{s}\simeq \dot k_{a}\approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$
in tissue at the focus, the usual peak synchrotron value.

A point of the object which is stepped through the beam is seen for

\begin{equation*}
t_{eff}=\frac{w_{x}}{v}=\frac{w_{x}}{\Delta x/t_{exp}}
       =\frac{w_{x}}{\Delta x}\,t_{exp}=0.02\ {\rm s},
\end{equation*}
the nominal dwell time quoted for the scan.  The dose actually
deposited in that point in the one pass is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{s}= \dot D_{s}\,t_{eff}
     \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
     \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}
per projection, i.e. $\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ at the
surface of a tissue with the mass energy absorption coefficient of air.
This is the $\approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous rate integrated
over the $\approx 20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell.  For a $n_{E}$-energy multiple-energy
acquisition the number simply scales as $n_{E}D_{s}$, e.g. $\approx7.7\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
for a conventional dual-energy acquisition and $\approx 11\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
for three energies, all essentially the air kerma quoted above.

3.text:
For a photon beam which is small compared with the cavity the energy which
is put into the cavity per unit time is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E = \dot N_{ip}W = \frac{I}{e}W
\end{equation*}

where $I$ is the ionisation current collected in the cavity, $e$ the
electronic charge and $W$ the mean energy required to produce an ion pair
in air, $W\simeq 33.97\;{\rm eV}$.  With

\begin{equation*}
I=2.0\times10^{-12}\ {\rm A}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}}{1.602\times10^{-19}}\,W
     =1.25\times10^{7}\,{\rm ip\,s^{-1}}\;W\simeq
     6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is deposited in the air which is simultaneously seen by the beam.  At
the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

so that, with the $15.1\,{\rm cm}$ long cavity,

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=0.03\times0.60\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm\,g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272\,
{\rm cm^{3}}\simeq3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is irradiated at any instant.  The air kerma
rate at the focus is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E}{m_{a}}
          =\frac{I\,W}{e\,m_{a}}
          \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}\;
            {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx0.2\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in air.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{{\rm tissue}}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{{\rm air}}$ the
same number is the absorbed-dose rate in soft tissue at the focus.  In
terms of the photon fluence rate $\dot\Phi$

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \dot\Phi\,E_{0}\,
\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}_{a}\;, \qquad
\dot\Phi =\frac{I\,W}{e\,E_{0}(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}m_{a}},
\end{equation*}

the familiar $I\,W/e\,m$ form.

A material point on the surface is in the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ wide slit only
for the transit time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}
\end{equation*}

with $v_{s}$ the horizontal scan speed.  In the notation of the
statement

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step}/{\rm exposure\,time}}\approx2.0\times
10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

is the dwell time of the slit on a given surface element.  The dose
received by that element during one passage of the slit, i.e. the dose
per energy, per image point, is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{p}= \dot K_{a}\,t_{d}
     =\frac{I\,W}{e\,m_{a}}\,
       \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}
     \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
     \approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

about $4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 33 keV exposure, $\sim 0.2\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
at the focus.  This is the familiar point dose for synchrotron scanning
radiography.  For an $N_{e}$–energy multi–energy set the dose scales
linearly with $N_{e}$.

If the dose is summed over the whole $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ field
which is imaged with a $0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ slit

\begin{equation*}
N_{s}\simeq\frac{50.0}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

slit positions are required.  The integrated, line–integrated energy
deposited on the $50\,{\rm mm}$ line is

\begin{equation*}
D_{{\rm line}}\simeq N_{s}D_{p}\approx1.7\times10^{2}\times3.9\times
10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per energy on the whole $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ field, i.e. an
average surface dose per unit area of the field which is the same
$D_{p}\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per image point.  The $0.6\!-\!0.7\,{\rm mGy}$
is the often quoted “cumulative’’ energy deposited in the scanned line
for a single photon energy.  Neglecting the $W$ – value uncertainty this
is $\approx3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point, $\approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
energy, essentially the dose quoted in the synchrotron scanning
literature.

2.text:
In an air ion chamber the charge produced in the air is

* $N_{i}=I/e$ ion pairs s$^{-1}$

and the energy taken out of the beam in the air in the chamber is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E=I\,W/e
\end{equation*}

with $W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}$ for air.  In the good absorption limit
the air kerma rate in the irradiated volume is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}=\frac{\dot E}{m_{air}}
          =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{air}A_{b}L}\;,
\end{equation*}

$A_{b}$ being the beam cross section which is actually irradiated and
$L$ the geometrical length of the chamber in the beam.

At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{b}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\,{\rm mm^{2}}
      =1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}A_{b}L
      =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
       \times15.1{\rm cm}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg} .
\end{equation*}

Hence

\begin{equation*}
\dot E = I\,W/e =2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
       \approx6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm W}}{3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}}
            \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the air kerma rate, and for photons $K\approx \dot D_{air}$, in the
focal spot.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{tissue}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$
the same number is the tissue kerma rate in the 0.30$\times$6 mm$^{2}$
focal spot,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq \Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}
             \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx
             0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. about $190\;\mu{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ in the beam.

For a horizontal raster the time a given surface element spends in the
$w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ focal spot is the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{scan}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t}
\end{equation*}

which is quoted as

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\equiv\frac{w_{f}}{\dot x}\approx 2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}\; .
\end{equation*}

The dose actually received by a surface element during one transit
through the beam is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot K_{air}\;t_{d}
     \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
     \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

$\approx 4\;\mu$Gy $\approx 4\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}$ per transit per
energy.  This is the conventional $\approx 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ 
focal kerma rate reduced by the $t_{d}\approx20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell time
of a point in the $0.3\;{\rm mm}$ spot.  In air-kerma units this is
$\approx0.44\;\mu$R per transit.  The number scales linearly with the
number $N_{E}$ of energies acquired per raster line, $D_{N}=N_{E}D_{1}$,
and with the conventional $W/e=33.97\;{\rm V}$ value for air.  This is
the $\sim\mu$Gy per pixel dose normally quoted for a synchrotron
multiple-energy Laue scan; the often quoted $\approx 2\times10^{-4}\ {\rm
Gy\,s^{-1}}$ focal dose rate is the same figure expressed as a rate.
∎

6.text:
In the air the kerma rate which is produced by the beam which is
illuminating the chamber is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}=\frac{{\rm d}E_{tr}/{\rm d}t}{m_{air}}=
\frac{\dot Q\,W}{m_{air}} ,
\end{equation*}

$W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}$  for air,  $\dot Q=I$.  For a small
$\mu_{en}L$ the mass of air which is actually ionised at the focus is the
mass of air contained in the beam

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=0.018\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{irr}=A_{f}L_{c}=0.018\times 15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{irr}
      =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272{\rm cm^{3}}
      \approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg} .
\end{equation*}

Hence in air

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}=\frac{I\,W}{m_{air}}
 =\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm J\,C^{-1}}}
        {3.52\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}}
 \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
 \approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at the focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{tissue}\simeq (\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$
the absorbed dose rate in soft tissue is essentially the air kerma rate,
$\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{air}$.  A material point of the subject is in the
horizontal focus

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\simeq\frac{w_{f}}{v_{scan}}
      \approx 0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

for the quoted dwell.  The dose delivered in one passage of the beam,
i.e. one mono-energetic exposure per pixel, is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}
     \approx 1.9\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times 2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
     \approx 3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

about $3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy and per pixel at the surface, i.e.
$\approx 4\times10^{-3}\,{\rm mGy}$ per mono-energetic point exposure.
With the usual $W_{air}=33.97\;{\rm J/C}$ this is

$D_{1}\approx I\,W\,t_{d}/m_{air}\approx3.8\!-\!4.5\,\mu{\rm Gy}$
per 33 keV exposure,

essentially the air kerma rate times the 20 ms dwell.  For a conventional
dual-energy, i.e. two mono-energetic acquisitions, the cumulative surface
dose per pixel is $\simeq 2D_{1}\approx 7\!-\!8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$, $\approx
12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for the three-energy set commonly used in synchrotron
multi-energy imaging, the nominal $\approx 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy being
the often quoted synchrotron “micro-dose” level.  The factor
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{tissue}/(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}\approx1$ has been used
to go from air kerma to tissue absorbed dose.  This is the instantaneous
peak dose per pixel; the time-averaged dose over the whole scanned field
is the same dose per dwell reduced by the beam-to-step duty cycle.

7.text:
In an air ionization chamber the charge collected per unit time is

* $I = 2.0\times10^{-12}\;{\rm C\,s^{-1}}$,

so that the number of ion pairs produced per second is $I/e$ and the
energy deposited in the gas per unit time is

\begin{equation*}
 \dot E_{a}= \frac{I}{e}\,W = I\,W/e
\end{equation*}

with $W\simeq 33.97\,{\rm eV}$ for air. 

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times 33.97\approx 6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm J\,s^{-1}} 
\end{equation*}

is the rate at which energy is absorbed in the volume of air which is
actually intercepted by the beam.  With the beam at the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and a sensitive length $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ along the beam the mass of air
seen by the beam is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a} A_{f}L
      =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.018\,{\rm cm^{2}}
       \times15.1\,{\rm cm}
      \approx 3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate in the beam, i.e. the energy absorbed per
unit mass per unit time in the beam when it is fully on the chamber, is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
       =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}\approx
       1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$
the photon fluence rate which gives this kerma is

\begin{equation*}
\dot\Phi =\frac{\dot K_{a}}{E_{\gamma}(\mu_{en}/\rho)}
       \approx\frac{1.93\times10^{-7}\;{\rm J\,g^{-1}s^{-1}}}
                    {5.29\times10^{-15}{\rm J}\times0.328{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}}
       \approx1.1\times10^{8}\;{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at 33.0 keV, $E_{\gamma}=5.29\times10^{-15}\,$J.  For tissue
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ the kerma rate in tissue
under the beam is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot\Phi\,E_{\gamma}\,
        \Bigl(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Bigr)_{t}
        \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,min^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

when the point is in the fully illuminated part of the beam.

For a horizontal line scan the time a given surface point spends in the
$w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ wide focal spot is the transit time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v}= \frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step}/{\rm exposure\;time}}
\end{equation*}

which is given in the statement as

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\approx 0.02\;{\rm s} .
\end{equation*}

Hence the energy absorbed per unit mass at a point which is traversed
once by the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx\dot D_{t}\,t_{d}
     \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}
     \approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. of the order of a few $\mu{\rm Gy}$ at the surface per 33 keV
pass.  In the usual three – energy Laue tomography this is

\begin{equation*}
D_{3E}\approx 3\,D_{1}\sim1\!-\!2\times10^{-5}\;{\rm Gy}\approx10\!-\!15\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

at the surface, essentially the same number being often quoted as
$\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy image, $ \dot K_{a}\approx
1.9\times10^{-4}\,{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ in the beam.  With the quoted
0.02 s dwell this is $\approx3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per single-energy line
scan, $ \approx 0.39\,\mu{\rm rad}$, the conventional air kerma being
practically identical to the tissue absorbed dose at 33 keV.  The
$ \sim 10^{-6}\!-\!10^{-5}\,$Gy level is the familiar synchrotron
micro-dose regime for a single focal pass; $N_{E}$ energies scale
linearly with $N_{E}$.  This is the peak surface dose; the mean dose
averaged over the whole scanned field is reduced by the duty factor
$w_{f}/{\rm field}$. ∎

